1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:11,323
It's a normal Tuesday. You're walking to work, coffee in hand, when your coworker steps out of the bakery with the worst haircut you have ever seen. It looks like someone put a bowl on his head and missed.

2
00:00:12,223 --> 00:00:13,899
Whoa! Love the new haircut!

3
00:00:14,249 --> 00:00:30,566
And the second the words leave your mouth, a number appears above your head. Four thousand, eight hundred and twelve. Then... tick... four thousand, eight hundred and thirteen. You just lied. And somehow... the universe noticed.

4
00:00:31,466 --> 00:00:41,290
By the end of this week, you'll find out that everyone has a number. Your sister. Your best friend. Your sweet little grandma. And one of those numbers is going to completely break your heart.

5
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:48,371
You wake up hoping it was a dream. It wasn't.

6
00:00:48,721 --> 00:00:53,753
Your sister Mia is pouring cereal. Above her head: two thousand, three hundred and one.

7
00:00:54,653 --> 00:00:58,716
Your dad walks in, reading the news on his phone. Eleven thousand, four hundred.

8
00:00:59,066 --> 00:01:00,964
Morning, champ. Slept great!

9
00:01:01,314 --> 00:01:04,570
He did not sleep great. Then your mom walks in right behind him.

10
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:07,381
Honey, I love the new shirt you bought.

11
00:01:07,731 --> 00:01:14,888
Nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-eight. Okay. So your parents' marriage is built on small, polite lies. Good to know.

12
00:01:15,788 --> 00:01:21,627
And then Grandma Rose walks in slowly with her cane, smiling like an angel. And above her head...

13
00:01:22,527 --> 00:01:27,401
Ninety-six THOUSAND. Grandma. The woman who knits you socks every Christmas.

14
00:01:28,301 --> 00:01:32,787
You look at the cat. Nothing. No number at all. Cats don't lie. They just don't care.

15
00:01:33,687 --> 00:01:44,178
You grab your phone and search: "Why can I see numbers above people's heads?" The first result says you need more sleep. The second result says you might be a wizard. Neither is helpful.

16
00:01:45,078 --> 00:01:51,223
Outside, it's chaos. Everyone has a number. The mailman: eight thousand. A jogger: twenty-two thousand.

17
00:01:52,123 --> 00:01:55,800
A little boy with chocolate all over his face, holding his mom's hand.

18
00:01:56,150 --> 00:01:57,290
Did you eat the candy?

19
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:58,165
No.

20
00:01:58,515 --> 00:02:00,238
Three. Nice try, kid.

21
00:02:01,138 --> 00:02:02,432
Sorry, we're out of oat milk!

22
00:02:02,782 --> 00:02:05,840
They are not out of oat milk. They just don't want to make it.

23
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:13,446
Across the street, the car salesman is shaking hands with a customer. Thirty-eight thousand, nine hundred. No surprise there.

24
00:02:14,346 --> 00:02:22,692
And on a TV screen in a shop window, a politician is giving a speech. His number doesn't even fit. The label just says... "ERROR."

25
00:02:23,592 --> 00:02:30,730
You need to test this properly. So you meet your best friend Jay at the park, and you ask him the most dangerous question a friend can ask.

26
00:02:31,630 --> 00:02:32,832
Do you like my new shoes?

27
00:02:33,182 --> 00:02:34,819
Yeah... they're great!

28
00:02:35,169 --> 00:02:39,626
Seven hundred and twelve, Jay. Wow. So you go home and write down everything you know.

29
00:02:45,926 --> 00:02:52,659
And just like that, you become a human lie detector. At work, your boss, Mr. Kent, stops by your desk.

30
00:02:53,009 --> 00:02:56,244
Great job this month. That raise is coming very soon.

31
00:02:56,594 --> 00:03:01,348
It is not coming soon. You quietly open a new tab and start updating your resume.

32
00:03:02,248 --> 00:03:08,237
On Saturday, you and Jay walk through the weekend market. A man at a stall is selling necklaces to an old lady.

33
00:03:09,137 --> 00:03:11,478
Real gold, ma'am. Only two hundred dollars.

34
00:03:11,828 --> 00:03:16,027
Fifty-one thousand lies, and he's adding more by the second. You step in.

35
00:03:16,927 --> 00:03:20,695
Sir, can you scratch it for us? Real gold doesn't change color.

36
00:03:21,045 --> 00:03:26,285
The old lady hugs you. Jay stares at you like you're a superhero. Honestly? You feel like one.

37
00:03:27,185 --> 00:03:29,413
That night is poker night with Jay's cousins.

38
00:03:29,763 --> 00:03:32,989
"I've got nothing," says one cousin. Tick. You fold.

39
00:03:33,339 --> 00:03:36,331
"I'm bluffing," says Jay. No tick. You fold again.

40
00:03:36,681 --> 00:03:41,317
By midnight, you've won forty-three dollars, and everyone thinks you're some kind of genius.

41
00:03:42,217 --> 00:03:48,088
Sunday, Jay has a first date. You sit two tables away, pretending to read a newspaper. Upside down.

42
00:03:48,988 --> 00:03:49,762
I'm a doctor.

43
00:03:50,112 --> 00:03:51,390
I love dogs.

44
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,004
I've never done this before.

45
00:03:53,904 --> 00:03:59,833
Jay leaves before dessert. On the walk home, you feel incredible. Nobody can fool you. Nobody.

46
00:04:03,733 --> 00:04:10,407
Thanks, man. But... it's kind of creepy how you always know. Like I can't say anything around you anymore.

47
00:04:12,957 --> 00:04:21,600
So you make a decision. If lying makes your number go up, then you just... won't lie anymore. Ever. How hard can it be? Very hard, it turns out.

48
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:27,729
At karaoke that Friday, Jay sings his heart out. It's the worst singing you have ever heard.

49
00:04:28,629 --> 00:04:29,878
So? How was I?

50
00:04:30,228 --> 00:04:33,421
Everyone looks at you. You open your mouth. Your number waits.

51
00:04:34,321 --> 00:04:37,249
Honestly? You sounded like a cat in a washing machine.

52
00:04:40,199 --> 00:04:45,650
Your number doesn't move. Not even one. You told the truth. So why does everyone suddenly hate you?

53
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:54,932
Then there's Mia. She's been quiet all week. Leaving the house every morning at eight, coming home at five. Same as always. But something's off.

54
00:04:55,832 --> 00:04:57,124
Hey... you okay?

55
00:04:57,474 --> 00:04:58,236
I'm fine.

56
00:04:58,586 --> 00:05:01,326
You push. And push. Until she finally breaks.

57
00:05:02,226 --> 00:05:10,121
I lost my job, okay? Three weeks ago. I've been sitting in the library all day, every day. I didn't want Mom to worry. Promise me you won't tell her.

58
00:05:11,021 --> 00:05:16,557
You promise. And you mean it. But at dinner the next night, Mom smiles across the table.

59
00:05:16,907 --> 00:05:18,263
So, Mia, how's work going?

60
00:05:18,613 --> 00:05:19,623
Busy, but good.

61
00:05:20,523 --> 00:05:26,116
And you just... can't stand it. You've spent all week catching lies. It's a reflex now.

62
00:05:26,466 --> 00:05:27,526
She got fired.

63
00:05:28,876 --> 00:05:34,715
Mom drops her fork. Dad stops chewing. Mia looks at you like you've stabbed her in the back, and walks away without a word.

64
00:05:35,615 --> 00:05:45,019
You look up at your own number. It didn't move. You told the truth. One hundred percent honest. So why does it feel like the worst thing you've ever done?

65
00:05:46,569 --> 00:05:59,457
You can't sleep. So you sneak downstairs, and Grandma Rose is still awake, doing a crossword. You sit next to her. You stare at her number. Ninety-six thousand, four hundred and forty. And finally, you ask.

66
00:06:00,357 --> 00:06:02,199
Grandma... have you ever lied?

67
00:06:02,549 --> 00:06:04,317
Oh, sweetheart. All the time.

68
00:06:05,217 --> 00:06:13,795
She tells you about Grandpa. For sixty years, every single morning, he'd fix his tie in the mirror and ask, "Rose, do I look handsome?"

69
00:06:14,695 --> 00:06:18,224
And every morning, I said yes. Even the morning he wore the orange tie.

70
00:06:19,124 --> 00:06:32,049
Twenty-one thousand mornings. Twenty-one thousand lies. And then there was the soup. Every Sunday, Grandpa made the same terrible soup. She ate every single bowl and called it delicious. That's another three thousand.

71
00:06:32,949 --> 00:06:40,088
The last one was in the hospital. He asked me if he was going to be okay. I held his hand and said, 'You'll be home by Sunday.'

72
00:06:40,988 --> 00:06:43,403
He wasn't. But he fell asleep smiling.

73
00:06:45,353 --> 00:06:53,568
You look at the number above her head. And for the first time, it doesn't look like a crime. It looks like sixty years of love... counted one by one.

74
00:06:55,118 --> 00:07:05,923
The next morning, you walk to the park to clear your head. And you see something you've never seen before. An old man, alone on a bench, feeding pigeons. Above his head: zero.

75
00:07:06,823 --> 00:07:11,631
Zero. Seventy years old, and not a single lie. You have to talk to him.

76
00:07:12,531 --> 00:07:14,371
Excuse me... have you ever told a lie?

77
00:07:14,721 --> 00:07:17,047
Never. Not once. Name's Walter.

78
00:07:17,397 --> 00:07:19,671
You expect him to be proud. He isn't.

79
00:07:20,571 --> 00:07:31,378
When my wife asked if I liked her paintings, I said no. When my son asked if I was proud of him, I said, 'Not yet.' He was nineteen. He hasn't called me in twenty years.

80
00:07:32,278 --> 00:07:38,638
Every word I ever said was true. And now there's no one left to hear them. The truth is a tool, kid. Not a weapon.

81
00:07:39,538 --> 00:07:43,247
You sit with him for a long time. Before you leave, you tell him one thing.

82
00:07:43,597 --> 00:07:48,472
Call your son. Tell him the truth. The real one. That you're proud of him.

83
00:07:51,822 --> 00:07:56,219
You go home and knock on Mia's door. She doesn't answer. So you talk through it.

84
00:07:57,119 --> 00:08:04,488
I'm sorry. It wasn't my secret to tell. I thought being honest made me a good person. It doesn't. Not on its own.

85
00:08:05,388 --> 00:08:09,341
Mom's actually being really nice about it. She's helping me with my resume.

86
00:08:09,691 --> 00:08:10,833
She crosses her arms.

87
00:08:11,183 --> 00:08:12,259
I'm still mad at you.

88
00:08:13,159 --> 00:08:15,085
No tick. She means it. Fair enough.

89
00:08:15,435 --> 00:08:18,488
...But I'm making dinner tonight. And you're eating it.

90
00:08:19,388 --> 00:08:31,311
Dinner is... a disaster. The pasta is somehow both burnt AND raw. The sauce is a color that does not exist in nature. Mia watches you take the first bite.

91
00:08:32,211 --> 00:08:33,698
So? How's my cooking?

92
00:08:34,048 --> 00:08:39,466
You think about Grandpa's orange tie. About Walter, alone on his bench. And you smile.

93
00:08:40,366 --> 00:08:42,024
...Perfect.

94
00:08:50,774 --> 00:09:05,806
That night, you look at your reflection. Four thousand, eight hundred and fourteen. And you finally understand. The number was never about how bad someone is. It's about how many times they had to choose between the truth... and someone they love.

95
00:09:06,706 --> 00:09:19,512
But then, a second number appears. Right below yours. A blue one. "Lies told to you: thirty-one thousand, five hundred and sixty." Which means somebody... has a lot of explaining to do.

96
00:09:20,412 --> 00:09:27,993
So here's the question. If you could see everyone's lie count, would you really want to know? Or would you rather keep believing the soup is delicious?

